# Seat: Typography — Round 7 (working notes, written as I go)

Build: commit 984a0f17, frozen. Lens: type scale, 400/600 only, tracking curve,
leading, measure, mono micro-labels, numerals, orphans/widows, hierarchy.

## Log

### 1. Tracking survey (driven, 1440, all 10 states)
Probed computed letterfit for every visible text node, grouped by product
scope + family + size. Most of the audit's 10 "tracking" groups dissolve on
inspection — the curves compose a polarity branch (`--tr-13 + --tr-rev` =
-0.002em on the dock's reversed "Add task"; `--tr-12 + --tr-rev` = +0.002em on
"1 overdue"), which the audit does not model. Those are principled, not drift.

What survives:
- tasks · Geist Mono 11: `.trayCount` "3" is tracked +0.1em (the uppercase
  micro-label step) while `.pacePct` "38%" is tracked -0.012em. Two mono
  FIGURES in the same header band, 0.112em apart in letterfit.
- notes · Geist 12: `button.groupBtn` ("What it is about", "When") computes
  `normal` — the only 12px control in Notes off `--tr-12`.

### 2. Timeline · the date row below the axis is a staircase (CONFIRMED, measured)
across layout, `timeline.owner-flight`, widths 1024/1280/1440/1920 (identical
numbers at all four):
  ABOVE the axis  `.b-copy` is anchored by `bottom:` → `.b-date` top = 623 for
    every moment, whether the title ran one line or two. One clean row.
  BELOW the axis  `.b-copy` is anchored by `top:` → `.b-date` top = 752 for the
    one-line titles ("Send the invitations", "Venue walk-through") and 774 for
    the two-line one ("Choose the evening music"). A 22px step in a row of
    dates that is otherwise a ruler's own scale.
src/across.css:266-277 is where the two anchors are set.
Fix: reserve two lines for `.b-title` on the across axis and hug the
axis-facing edge, so `.b-copy` has one height on both sides.

### 3. tasks · the wrapping law — RAISED AND WITHDRAWN
tasks.css:923 gives `.cardTitle` two wrapping laws switched by a sibling
(`:has(.when)` → pretty, otherwise balance), four lines under a comment that
writes "THE RULE: no text block may have two wrapping laws switched by a
sibling." I measured both laws over 9 state×width combinations (board/dense/
compact × 1280/1440/1920), 142 multi-line titles:
  as-is             18 one-word LAST lines, 2 one-word FIRST lines, 0 over-clamp
  balance everywhere 8 one-word LAST lines, 9 one-word FIRST lines, 0 over-clamp
  pretty everywhere 24 one-word LAST lines, 14 short last lines
Balance-everywhere halves the widows but produces exactly the rag the comment
predicts at 1280 dense: «Chase» ⏎ «linen order, now» ⏎ «shipping Tuesday»,
«Gravel» ⏎ «delivered for» ⏎ «the top car park», five of them in one column.
So the recorded decision is the better of the two blunt options and my
re-measurement CONFIRMS it. NOT raised as a finding.
(What remains visible: 13 of the 18 widows are on chipped titles under
`pretty` — "Clear Sunday 11am late checkout with / housekeeping" breaks after
a preposition at 1280/1440/1920, and "Build the Saturday / run-sheet" at 1440.
Content-shaped, not fixable by a keyword.)

### 4. `.pacePct` — the one inherited letterfit in the suite (CONFIRMED)
Swept every visible text element in all 10 states for a letter-spacing
inherited across a size OR family change. Exactly one hit, suite-wide:
  tasks `span.pacePct` "38%" — Geist Mono 11px, ls = -0.13px, inherited from
  `.headFacts` (Geist 13px, `--tr-13` = -0.010em). So a MONO figure carries
  -0.0118em, a letterfit computed for 13px SANS.
tasks.css:325-328 declares family, size, line-height, weight, colour and
tabular figures for `.pacePct` and no letter-spacing. Tasks has no mono step;
the suite's declared one is +0.04em (notes `--tr-key`, timeline `--track-data`).
Meanwhile `.trayCount` (tasks.css:149) is a mono figure at +0.1em with
`margin-right:-0.1em` to kill the trailing space — so the two mono figures in
the same header band sit 0.112em apart in fit.

### 5. HEADLINE — one role, five settings: the uppercase micro-label (CONFIRMED)
Swept every uppercase string in all 10 states at 1440. One typographic role —
the small-caps eyebrow that names a region — is set FIVE ways in one app:

  notes     Geist SANS  11px  600  +0.075em   FILING UNDER · OTHER WAYS IN ·
                                              YOUR NOTES · STILL TO DECIDE ·
                                              HOW IT ARRIVED · TURN THIS INTO…
  notes     Geist SANS  12px  600  +0.075em   LISTENING
  tasks     Geist Mono  11px  400  +0.10em    TO DO · IN PROGRESS · REVIEW ·
                                              WAITING · DONE · VENUE · BAR
  timeline  Geist Mono  11px  400  +0.16em    DAYS AWAY · BEHIND YOU · ACROSS ·
                                              TODAY IS 16 JULY · ONE OF THREE
  timeline  Geist Mono  12px  400  +0.20em    MARA & FINN

Two families, two weights, four tracks. Notes is the outlier that costs most:
it is the only product whose eyebrow is SANS, and the only one at 600 — so in
Notes weight does NOT separate a label from a title (both 600) while in Tasks
and Timeline it does (400 vs 600). The same role therefore carries different
information in different rooms of one application, and the rail does not move
between them, so the label is one of the few things that visibly re-skins when
a person crosses the spine.

This is not the ratified-tracking exclusion. Three ratified TRACKING curves do
not license two FAMILIES and two WEIGHTS for one role.

Tested the unification (Geist Mono / 400 / +0.16em applied to every uppercase
micro-label) at 390/768/1280/1440/1920 on notebook, board, dense, review,
voice: 0 labels wrapped to a new line, 0 clipped, 0 new sideways scroll; the
largest single growth is 24.4px on "STILL TO DECIDE", which sits alone on a
rule with room. Safe.
Precedent for the fix already exists in this build: round 6 hoisted the keycap
to ONE declaration in shell.css (11px mono, +0.04em) and left the products only
their ink. The eyebrow is the same move, one object later.

### 6. notes · the tracking curve is indexed off the type ramp (CONFIRMED)
notes.css:89-90 declares the ramp at 11 12 13 15 17 20 27 34.
notes.css:106-108 declares the curve at 11 12 14 16 18 21 27 34.
Four of the eight ramp sizes — 13, 15, 17, 20 — have NO step of their own, so
every rule at those sizes borrows a neighbour by hand, and the file borrows
different neighbours for the same size. Measured in the file:
  --t-17 → --tr-18 (-0.021em), --tr-16 (-0.016em) AND --tr-21 (-0.026em)
  --t-15 → --tr-16 and --tr-14
  --t-13 → --tr-14 and --tr-12
against a token block that says "One tracking curve, by size, declared once.
Every letter-spacing in this file is a token from it."
Visible instance, driven (lift a note → select → peel → send): the receipt sets
the picked sentence "Ceremony 2pm in the orchard, drinks on the" at 17px/600,
letter-spacing -0.272px (-0.016em, `.peelSent`, notes.css:1289 region) directly
under the SAME sentence on the desk at 17px/400, -0.357px (-0.021em). The
heavier setting is the LOOSER one, which is backwards.
Fix: re-index the curve onto the ramp — declare --tr-13/-15/-17/-20 on the
existing curve and replace the borrowed references, keeping --tr-cap and
--tr-key as ROLE steps outside the size index.

### 7. tasks · the phone list's measure is starved by a column it isn't using (CONFIRMED)
tasks.css:573-580, the ≤720 fold: `.lrows { grid-template-columns: 34px
minmax(0,1fr) 74px }`, `.lrowSay` in column 2 row 1, `.when` in column 3 row 1,
`.lrowMeta` in column 3 row 2. `.lrow .when[data-t="none"]` (line 563) is
`visibility: hidden` — so a row with NO day still hands 74px of row 1 to an
invisible chip.
Measured, driven, tasks.board:
  @320  `.lrowSay` = 116px → ~14 characters per line at 13px
  @390  `.lrowSay` = 186px → ~22 characters per line at 13px (row is 322px)
  @430  `.lrowSay` = 226px → ~27 characters per line
5 of 13 rows in board (17 of 32 in dense) carry no day at all, and pay the
74px anyway. The desk already solved this exact problem with a float —
tasks.css:911-918 argues it at length ("the date chip was stealing width from
all three lines instead of one") — and the phone re-introduces it as a grid
reserve.
Validated fix (driven, no overlap, no sideways scroll at 320/390/430):
  @media (max-width:720px){
    [data-app="tasks"] .lrow:not(:has(.when:not([data-t="none"]))) .lrowSay
      { grid-column: 2 / -1; }
  }
  → say column 116→198 (@320), 186→268 (@390), 226→308 (@430);
    13 fewer wrapped lines @320, 5 @390, 7 in dense@390.
The chipped rows keep the narrow measure on lines 2+ until the chip becomes a
float; that is the second half and it is a DOM change, not a CSS one.
